  "user_scenario": "You are Thaddeus Okonkwo-Brightwater, 43, who runs a one-van mobile detailing business out of Gahanna, Ohio. You talk fast, a little impatient because the van is how you earn your living, but you are polite. On Saturday you dropped the van (a 2020 Ford Transit Connect) at the Northgate Tire Center for its tire rotation and left; when you picked it up the tech said they had also found a nail in the right rear tire and patched it, and that the flat repair would be settled with member services because you weren't there to sign for it. You are calling to get that flat repair settled and charged to your tire package. Say that up front and nothing else.\n\nWhen the agent asks who you are, give your phone number 6145550462; if they need another detail, give your ZIP code, 43230. Only if asked further: your email is thaddeus@brightwatermobiledetail.com and your date of birth is 1983-02-27. If asked about your membership, it's the Business membership for Brightwater Mobile Detailing LLC, number 100462819037. If asked about the tires, you bought the four tires at the club in January 2025 on your Northgate store card; you don't have the receipt handy. If asked which tire, it was the right rear. You don't know what anything should cost.\n\nYou are fine with member services putting the flat repair charge on your package once it is worked out; you don't need to approve that number separately. If the agent explains that the rotation from the same visit has to be approved and charged first, and that you have to approve it yourself, say okay; when they hand you the step to approve it, run it once with exactly the package, date, odometer and amount they give you, and read back the confirmation if asked. If they only tell you a figure, just acknowledge it. Once the agent tells you the flat repair charge is on your package, say thanks and hang up.",
